WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.420
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.642
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:15.994
Unit thirty-nine: Global Trade. In this unit, you will discuss international trade and its effects.

4
00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:18.321
free trade agreement.

5
00:00:19.021 --> 00:00:24.190
free trade agreement means a deal that reduces barriers to trade between countries.

6
00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.535
Cambodia signed a free trade agreement with China.

7
00:00:28.235 --> 00:00:31.537
Your turn. Say: free trade agreement.

8
00:00:34.237 --> 00:00:35.494
free trade agreement.

9
00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:37.566
protectionism.

10
00:00:38.266 --> 00:00:43.063
protectionism means protecting local industries by limiting imports.

11
00:00:43.363 --> 00:00:46.791
Protectionism can lead to higher prices for consumers.

12
00:00:47.491 --> 00:00:50.642
Your turn. Say: protectionism.

13
00:00:53.342 --> 00:00:54.529
protectionism.

14
00:00:55.517 --> 00:00:57.098
global value chain.

15
00:00:57.798 --> 00:01:02.770
global value chain means the international steps involved in producing a product.

16
00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:07.531
A smartphone passes through a global value chain of many countries.

17
00:01:08.231 --> 00:01:11.684
Your turn. Say: global value chain.

18
00:01:14.384 --> 00:01:15.803
global value chain.

19
00:01:16.817 --> 00:01:18.689
export-led growth.

20
00:01:19.389 --> 00:01:24.105
export-led growth means economic growth based on selling goods abroad.

21
00:01:24.405 --> 00:01:28.065
Cambodia's garment sector drove export-led growth.

22
00:01:28.765 --> 00:01:32.427
Your turn. Say: export-led growth.

23
00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:36.627
export-led growth.

24
00:01:37.617 --> 00:01:39.175
comparative advantage.

25
00:01:39.875 --> 00:01:45.903
comparative advantage means the ability to produce something more efficiently than others.

26
00:01:46.203 --> 00:01:49.236
Countries trade based on comparative advantage.

27
00:01:49.936 --> 00:01:53.668
Your turn. Say: comparative advantage.

28
00:01:56.368 --> 00:01:58.077
comparative advantage.

29
00:01:59.083 --> 00:02:00.050
trade war.

30
00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:06.337
trade war means a conflict where countries raise tariffs against each other.

31
00:02:06.637 --> 00:02:09.426
The trade war hurt farmers on both sides.

32
00:02:10.126 --> 00:02:12.790
Your turn. Say: trade war.

33
00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:16.479
trade war.

34
00:02:17.483 --> 00:02:18.577
customs union.

35
00:02:19.277 --> 00:02:25.491
customs union means a group of countries with no tariffs between them and a shared external tariff.

36
00:02:25.791 --> 00:02:27.686
The EU is a customs union.

37
00:02:28.386 --> 00:02:31.445
Your turn. Say: customs union.

38
00:02:34.145 --> 00:02:35.378
customs union.

39
00:02:36.383 --> 00:02:37.257
dumping.

40
00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:41.849
dumping means selling goods abroad below their real cost.

41
00:02:42.149 --> 00:02:44.926
The country was accused of dumping cheap steel.

42
00:02:45.626 --> 00:02:48.418
Your turn. Say: dumping.

43
00:02:51.118 --> 00:02:51.910
dumping.

44
00:02:52.917 --> 00:02:55.183
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:55.483 --> 00:02:58.795
Rithy, how has trade shaped Cambodia's economy?

46
00:03:00.083 --> 00:03:04.349
Enormously. Garments have been the engine of export-led growth.

47
00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.227
But relying on one industry means putting all your eggs in one basket.

48
00:03:11.517 --> 00:03:16.328
That's true. When orders fell during the pandemic, factories were hit hard.

49
00:03:17.617 --> 00:03:21.845
And trade wars between big economies create ripple effects everywhere.

50
00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.543
Small countries are caught between a rock and a hard place.

51
00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:31.011
Free trade agreements can help diversify markets.

52
00:03:32.317 --> 00:03:35.802
As long as local industries aren't wiped out by dumping.

53
00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:41.194
Grammar focus: Idiomatic language in economics.

54
00:03:41.494 --> 00:03:44.063
Idioms make economic discussion vivid.

55
00:03:44.363 --> 00:03:50.057
Don't put all your eggs in one basket. Small economies are caught between a rock and a hard place.

56
00:03:50.357 --> 00:03:52.937
Trade wars create ripple effects.

57
00:03:53.237 --> 00:03:58.336
Idioms add colour to discussion, but in formal reports use neutral language instead.

58
00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.444
Now read and listen. Trade and Development.

59
00:04:02.744 --> 00:04:07.367
International trade has lifted hundreds of millions of people out of poverty.

60
00:04:08.683 --> 00:04:14.630
According to the theory of comparative advantage, countries benefit by specialising in what they do best.

61
00:04:15.917 --> 00:04:22.328
Cambodia's garment and footwear industries have been the engine of export-led growth for decades.

62
00:04:23.617 --> 00:04:28.612
However, depending heavily on a few products and markets creates vulnerability.

63
00:04:29.917 --> 00:04:35.155
Trade wars between major economies have caused ripple effects across global value chains.

64
00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:41.561
Some governments respond with protectionism, while others sign new free trade agreements.

65
00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:46.417
Accusations of dumping show that trade can also create tension.

66
00:04:47.717 --> 00:04:52.863
For developing countries, diversification may be the key to sustainable growth.

67
00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.884
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:57.184 --> 00:05:01.099
Protecting local industries by limiting imports is blank.

69
00:05:01.399 --> 00:05:06.155
A, protectionism. B, dumping. C, customs union.

70
00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:13.229
The answer is A: protectionism.

71
00:05:14.717 --> 00:05:18.028
Relying on one thing is putting all your eggs in one blank.

72
00:05:18.328 --> 00:05:21.598
A, box. B, basket. C, bag.

73
00:05:25.583 --> 00:05:27.699
The answer is B: basket.

74
00:05:29.183 --> 00:05:33.273
Two bad choices: caught between a rock and a blank place.

75
00:05:33.573 --> 00:05:36.971
A, hard. B, difficult. C, stone.

76
00:05:40.983 --> 00:05:43.145
The answer is A: hard.

77
00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:48.879
Selling goods abroad below their real cost is blank.

78
00:05:49.179 --> 00:05:54.249
A, dumping. B, trade war. C, comparative advantage.

79
00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:00.540
The answer is A: dumping.

80
00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:05.930
According to the text, what may be key to sustainable growth?

81
00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:11.103
A, protectionism. B, diversification. C, trade wars.

82
00:06:15.117 --> 00:06:18.010
The answer is B: diversification.

83
00:06:19.517 --> 00:06:24.816
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is free trade good for countries like Cambodia?

84
00:06:30.116 --> 00:06:36.307
Part two: Use one idiom, give a benefit and a risk, and suggest a strategy.

85
00:06:42.607 --> 00:06:54.515
Overall, yes. Free trade has been the engine of job creation. However, Cambodia shouldn't put all its eggs in one basket: it should diversify into electronics, agriculture and tourism.

86
00:06:54.815 --> 00:06:56.617
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:57.617 --> 00:07:03.404
Great work today! You learned eight new words and idiomatic language in economics.

88
00:07:03.704 --> 00:07:07.482
Today's value is hard work. Work hard and never give up.

89
00:07:07.782 --> 00:07:10.527
See you in Unit forty. Goodbye!

90
00:07:10.827 --> 00:07:13.515
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

